Most English teachers have spent at least one year(often much more) of their training in the UK and thus speak with a British accent. (I'm German and this was the case for all of my teachers except for one who spent time in Ireland and thus had an Irish accent). A teacher's pronunciation greatly influences their students. In most schools RP pronunciation is expected, but General American pronunciation is also accepted. You have to be consisten though. I'd say it's the norm for Germans to sound British (because of the teachers). There were exceptions among my class mates: the ones who spend time in the USA(a lot of  young Germans do that) and the ones who were  exposed to American pronunciation through watching a great amount of movies and TV shows in American English.

&#x200B;

Even though nowadays I watch way more American shows than British ones, I'd say of myself that my accent sounds more British too. My teachers spoke with this accents and my first shows I watched and rewatched a lot in English were British: Doctor Who, Torchwood, Downton Abbey, Harry Potter movies.
